  {
    "title": "Investigating a Hypothetical RAG Retrieval Miss: Where to Start?",
    "body": "Consider a hypothetical RAG experiment where a query, designed to retrieve a specific answer from a collection of 40 documents, failed to do so. The target document, containing the known answer (F1), was not present in the retrieved set (F2). This scenario is purely hypothetical and does not represent measured results.\n\nWithout further information, it's unclear whether the root cause lies in how the documents were processed (chunking), how the data was organized for searching (indexing), or how the question was phrased (query wording) (F3).\n\nThis post outlines a structured approach to investigating such a hypothetical retrieval failure. We'll focus on systematic checks that could be performed in a local RAG environment. The goal is to identify the most probable points of failure and discuss methodical ways to test them. What are the first few things to examine when a retrieval system unexpectedly misses its target document, especially when the system's internal workings are not fully transparent?",
    "fact_refs": ["F1", "F2", "F3"],
    "plan": "Describe a hypothetical RAG retrieval failure scenario. Outline initial diagnostic steps for users facing such issues, focusing on chunking, indexing, and query formulation. Maintain the hypothetical status throughout. Encourage discussion on systematic debugging for local RAG systems."
  }
